Wait, let me think about this more carefully. The `/*#` trick is:
- In C: `/*` starts a comment, and `#` is just part of the comment


- In Python: `#` starts a comment, and `import` is part of that comment line, so it gets ignored

Actually, I need to reconsider the structure. When C sees `/*#`, it begins a multi-line comment that continues until `*/`. Python, on the other hand, treats `#` as a comment marker, so everything after it on that line is ignored. This means the Python code can sit inside what looks like a C comment block, and both languages will interpret it correctly—the C compiler sees it as a comment, while Python executes it as code.

The pattern is to open with `/*#` to start the C comment and Python comment simultaneously, place the Python code in the middle, close with `*/` to end the C comment, then add `#*/` so Python treats the closing delimiter as a comment too.

When C processes this, it sees `/*` beginning a comment, then `#` and everything else until `*/` is ignored. Python sees `#` as a comment start, so `import sys...#*/` all gets skipped on that line. The C code that follows then executes normally.

